Connect ocean-floor processes with sediments and a changing geological record (@geo-oceans: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @geology, #source "../sources/Physical-Geology-Earle.lingua#r_5GWEYVQGR176TXMQT0S881ZSKX", #source "../sources/Introduction-to-Historical-Geology.lingua#r_1JKSSECEDW0M9QG30TJ1D9KHZD", #source "../sources/International-Stratigraphic-Guide.lingua#r_6NAYJE2KFXGRNCWP6TEY7AZE7J", #geology "Arid-landscapes-wind-and-episodic-water.lingua#r_3ZTA4T8X78Y6M415TA3BPH8TA0") { r_2EJ3CP09GCFYPWCM2KZK7H2QHK
**Read:** [Physical Geology, second edition — Steven Earle](../sources/Physical-Geology-Earle.lingua#r_5GWEYVQGR176TXMQT0S881ZSKX), chapter 18 in full; [Introduction to Historical Geology — Estrada and contributing authors](../sources/Introduction-to-Historical-Geology.lingua#r_1JKSSECEDW0M9QG30TJ1D9KHZD), the complete marine-sedimentary and proxy/history sections used; [International Stratigraphic Guide, abridged version — ICS](../sources/International-Stratigraphic-Guide.lingua#r_6NAYJE2KFXGRNCWP6TEY7AZE7J), correlation and unit relationships for the chosen record.

Ocean basins connect tectonic creation/destruction, water circulation, biological production, chemical conditions, and sediment transport. Bathymetry records several processes and changing histories; it is not explained by water depth alone. Oceanic crustal age, cooling, sediment cover, ridges, trenches, and margins provide different constraints.

Marine sediment includes terrigenous, biological, chemical, and volcanic components whose production, transport, dissolution, dilution, and preservation vary. A percentage change in one component can reflect another component's input. An accumulation rate needs an age model, density/compaction account, and an identified interval.

Cores provide samples of a record with possible gaps, disturbance, mixing, reworking, and uncertain correlation. A proxy is a measurement linked to a target through a physical/chemical/biological model; more precise instrumental measurement does not remove all environmental ambiguity.

**Work:** read a published core/section interpretation with its methods and age model. Separate observations, derived quantities, calibration, and environmental inference. Compare two explanations for a component/proxy change and identify an independent constraint. Explain how missing time or changing sedimentation would alter a claimed rate or sequence.

Read the whole sea-floor chapter and the relevant historical record treatment. This prepares climate and Earth-history reasoning as well as subsea infrastructure/resource questions. The later ocean, paleoclimate, and geochemistry subjects deepen sampling, circulation, proxy calibration, and quantitative reconstruction.

